</table>
<p>FED STD 209E was superseded by ISO 14644-1 but the class numbers remain in common use. CNC = Controlled Not Classified.</p>
<h2 id="gmp-heading">cGMP Compliance for Conveyors</h2>
<p>Current Good Manufacturing Practice (cGMP) under FDA 21 CFR Parts 210 and 211 requires that pharmaceutical equipment be designed, constructed, and maintained to facilitate cleaning, prevent contamination, and support process validation. For conveyors, cGMP compliance means:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Materials of construction:</strong> 316L stainless steel for product-contact frames. FDA-approved polymers (UHMW-PE, Delrin, silicone) for belt surfaces and wear strips. Material certificates (mill certs) for all metal components.</li>
<li><strong>Surface finish:</strong> Product-contact surfaces polished to 0.8 μm Ra (32 microinch) or better. Electropolishing preferred for corrosion resistance and cleanability. Similar requirements to <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary food conveyors</a> but with additional documentation requirements.</li>
<li><strong>Documentation package:</strong> Design Qualification (DQ), Factory Acceptance Test (FAT), Installation Qualification (IQ), Operational Qualification (OQ), and Performance Qualification (PQ) protocols. Component traceability, weld maps, and surface finish test reports.</li>
<li><strong>Change control:</strong> Any modification to the conveyor after validation requires a formal change control process. Even belt replacement must follow documented procedures with material verification.</li>
</ul>
<p>The documentation burden for pharmaceutical conveyors adds significant cost ($$$ to $$$$) over equivalent industrial conveyors. Budget 20-40% of hardware cost for validation documentation and qualification testing. For cost factors, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="design-heading">Cleanroom Conveyor Design</h2>
<p>Cleanroom conveyor design focuses on minimizing particle generation, enabling thorough cleaning, and preventing cross-contamination between products. Key design features:</p>
<h3>Particle Control</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Enclosed drives:</strong> Motors and gearboxes mount inside sealed housings. Drive belts or chains run inside enclosed channels. No exposed moving parts that generate or distribute particles.</li>
<li><strong>Low-particulate bearings:</strong> Sealed bearings with clean-grease fill. Self-lubricating polymer bearings eliminate lubricant-generated particles entirely.</li>
<li><strong>Smooth belt surfaces:</strong> Belt materials that do not generate fiber, flake, or dust. PU (polyurethane) belts with smooth, sealed surfaces are standard. No fabric-backed belts that shed fibers.</li>
<li><strong>Minimal fasteners:</strong> Reduce exposed bolt heads, screw threads, and crevices that trap particles and resist cleaning.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Cleaning and Changeover</h3>
<p>Pharmaceutical conveyors must support cleaning between product batches to prevent cross-contamination. Cleaning validation demonstrates that the cleaning procedure removes product residue to below acceptable limits. Conveyor design features that support cleaning validation:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tool-free belt removal:</strong> Belts remove for cleaning or inspection without tools. Quick-release tensioners and lift-up tail designs.</li>
<li><strong>Crevice-free construction:</strong> No dead legs, no hidden cavities, no trapped air spaces where residue accumulates.</li>
<li><strong>Wipeable surfaces:</strong> All external surfaces smooth and wipeable with IPA (isopropyl alcohol) or validated cleaning agents.</li>
<li><strong>WIP (Wash-in-Place):</strong> For contained conveyors handling HPAPIs, integrated spray nozzles clean internal surfaces without opening the containment barrier.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="containment-heading">Containment Conveying for Potent Compounds</h2>
<p>Highly potent active pharmaceutical ingredients (HPAPIs) with occupational exposure limits (OELs) below 10 μg/m3 require containment conveying. The conveyor operates inside an enclosed system, isolator, glovebox, or containment housing, that prevents operator exposure to airborne drug particles.</p>
<p>Containment conveyor features include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Enclosed housing:</strong> The conveyor runs inside a sealed enclosure with HEPA-filtered exhaust maintaining negative pressure relative to the room.</li>
<li><strong>Gloveport access:</strong> Operators interact with the conveyor through gloveports in the enclosure wall for belt changes, jam clearing, and maintenance.</li>
<li><strong>Split-butterfly valves:</strong> Product transfers into and out of the contained conveyor use split-butterfly valves (SBVs) that maintain containment during connection and disconnection.</li>
<li><strong>WIP/CIP systems:</strong> Integrated wash nozzles clean the conveyor and enclosure interior without breaking containment. Wash water drains to a contained waste system.</li>
<li><strong>Continuous liner systems:</strong> Waste product and cleaning materials discharge through continuous liners that seal contaminated material without exposure.</li>
</ul>
<p>Containment conveyor systems cost significantly more ($$$$) than standard pharmaceutical conveyors due to the enclosure, filtration, validation, and containment testing requirements. OEL testing (operator exposure monitoring) during commissioning verifies containment performance.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Pharmaceutical Conveyor Applications</h2>
<h3>Oral Solid Dosage (OSD)</h3>
<p>Tablet and capsule lines use conveyors between the tablet press or capsule filler, metal detector, deduster, coating pan, and packaging. Belt conveyors with smooth polyurethane belts transport loose tablets. Enclosed <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">bucket elevators</a> or <a href="/conveyor-types/vacuum-conveyors/">vacuum conveyors</a> handle vertical transfers. <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Indexing conveyors</a> position bottles under tablet counters and cappers.</p>
<h3>Aseptic Fill-Finish</h3>
<p>Vial, syringe, and cartridge filling lines operate in ISO Class 5 environments. Conveyors transport containers from the washer through depyrogenation tunnels to fill-finish machines. Pallet transfer (workpiece carrier) conveyors provide the precision positioning needed for robotic filling and stoppering. These conveyors run inside laminar airflow zones with HEPA-filtered ceiling panels.</p>
<h3>Pharmaceutical Packaging</h3>
<p>Pharmaceutical packaging lines use conveyors between blister packers, cartoners, bundlers, serialization stations, and <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/">case packers</a>. Serialization (track-and-trace) requirements add vision inspection stations and <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject conveyors</a> at each serialization point. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation conveyors</a> buffer between machines to maintain line efficiency.</p>
<h2 id="mfg-heading">Pharmaceutical Conveyor Manufacturers</h2>
<p>Pharmaceutical conveyor manufacturers range from general-purpose conveyor companies with cleanroom options to specialized pharmaceutical equipment builders:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dorner:</strong> Cleanroom-rated 2200 and 3200 series conveyors. FDA-compliant belting. ISO Class 7/8 capable.</li>
<li><strong>mk North America:</strong> Modular aluminum and stainless steel conveyor profiles. VersaMove pallet transfer for assembly and fill-finish.</li>
<li><strong>FlexLink:</strong> Modular chain conveyor platforms for pharmaceutical packaging. Stainless steel and aluminum options.</li>
<li><strong>Bosch Rexroth:</strong> TS 2plus pallet transfer system for vial and syringe assembly. Precision indexing for fill-finish lines.</li>
<li><strong>NCC Automated Systems:</strong> Custom pharmaceutical conveyor systems with full validation documentation packages.</li>
</ul>
<p>Manufacturer data from publicly available information. See our <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/">manufacturer directory</a> for a complete list.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What conveyors are used in pharmaceutical manufacturing?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Cleanroom-rated belt conveyors, pallet transfer systems, vacuum conveyors, and packaging line conveyors. All must meet <a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/cleanroom/">ISO cleanroom classifications</a> and cGMP requirements for materials, documentation, and validation.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What ISO class do pharma conveyors need?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Aseptic filling: ISO 5 (Class 100). Oral solid dosage: ISO 7-8. Packaging: ISO 8 or CNC. The conveyor must not generate particles that violate the room classification.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is GMP compliance for conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Validated materials (316L SS, FDA polymers), documented DQ/IQ/OQ/PQ qualification, material certificates, surface finish reports, weld records, and change control procedures. Documentation typically adds 20-40% to hardware cost.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What is containment conveying?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Containment conveying transports HPAPIs inside enclosed, HEPA-filtered systems to prevent operator exposure. Uses isolators, gloveboxes, split-butterfly valves, and WIP cleaning. Required when OELs fall below 10 μg/m3.</p>

</div>
